- op: create  # 893f
  at: '2026-08-29T09:44:46Z'  # 893f
  by: agent  # 893f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 893f
  clock: 1  # 893f
  nonce: 893f  # 893f
  data:  # 893f
    kind: work_item  # 893f
    title: "A library-signature catalogue (producing function -> returned type): 820 method-kind io rows across 9 languages need a typed receiver, and receivers from library return values cannot be typed from analysed source at all"  # 893f
    status: todo_hard  # 893f
    priority: 2  # 893f
    parent:  # 893f
    tags:  # 893f
    - io_boundary  # 893f
    - catalogue  # 893f
    - analysis_quality  # 893f
    isbefore: []  # 893f
    duplicate_of: []  # 893f
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 893f
    pr_ref:  # 893f
    description: "FILED FROM WI-doluf's cross-language check, which the owner asked for before\nchoosing the shape of a fix. The check answered the shape question and turned up\na larger hole than the one it was run for.\n\nTHE DATA. Method-kind io_primitives rows, deduped for catalogue inheritance\n(_CATALOG_PARENTS: kotlin/scala -> java, cpp -> c, elixir -> erlang). Every one\nof these needs a TYPED RECEIVER before it can ever match:\n\n  lang         own method rows   own receiver types   ADR-0006 'Chained'\n  python              184                26           NO  (Phase 4)\n  java                139                36           NO  (Phase 1, never landed)\n  objc                123                18           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  rust                118                26           YES (Phase 3 / WI-titor)\n  swift               100                31           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  javascript           48                13           NO  (n/a)\n  go                   45                16           YES (Phase 1; WI-doluf)\n  kotlin               42                 3           NO  (Phase 2)\n  scala                21                 5           NOT IN THE TABLE\n  TOTAL               820\n\nMethod rows are 97% of java's catalogue, 97% of kotlin's, 95% of objc's, 93% of\nswift's, 89% of scala's. For those languages, receiver typing is not one code\npath among many -- it is the precondition for almost the whole catalogue.\n\ncsharp is the clean negative: it has an analyzer and NO io_primitives catalogue,\nso it needs nothing here. Recording that so the next sweep does not re-derive it.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING, PRECISELY. The registries are built from ANALYSED DECLARATIONS,\nso a receiver typed from a library function's return value cannot be typed at\nall -- there is no declaration in the repo to register. Measured on go:\n`ln, _ := net.Listen(...)` then `ln.Accept()` emits dst go:external:0-0:Accept,\nand the catalogue's net.Listener.Accept row is unreachable no matter how correct\nit is. The missing input is one fact per producing function: what type does it\nreturn.\n\nWHY A SEPARATE CATALOGUE AND NOT A `returns:` KEY ON io_primitives ROWS.\nConsidered and rejected on three grounds, in order of weight:\n\n  1. It is a SIGNATURE fact, not an I/O fact. Putting \"what type does this\n     return\" inside a file whose declared job is \"what boundary does this cross\"\n     is one name carrying two meanings -- the shape INV-tutar cost 134\n     misclassified rows on, and the shape ADR-0049 was written to rule.\n  2. The CONSUMER IS NOT THE IO CATALOGUE. It is `var_types` in nine analyzers,\n     which also feeds slice quality, centrality, dead-code and taint. Scoping\n     the data to io rows would under-serve its own users, and the receiver types\n     an io row needs are a SUBSET of the ones a slice needs.\n  3. ONE table shape serves nine languages; an io_primitives extension is nine\n     parallel additions to nine files, each able to drift.\n\nSHAPE: key it the way the existing return-type registries already are\n(`Receiver.Method` / bare function name -> returned type), so per-language\nmachinery consumes it with NO new resolution path. WI-doluf proved that path end\nto end in go: with the qualifier preserved, an in-repo factory returning net.Conn\nnow classifies net.Conn.Read where it previously matched nothing.\n\nSEQUENCING NOTE. This is NOT the shortest path to unblocking INV-kanuk -- the\nADR-0049 disclosure boundary (WI-nosah) is, and it needs no new data. This is\nthe path that additionally FIXES RECALL: it makes go's, java's and the rest's\nreal receive surfaces reachable rather than merely stopping the false ones from\nminting. The two are complementary and neither waits on the other.\n\nSUB-GAP WORTH ITS OWN LOOK: scala / objc / swift are absent from ADR-0006's\nsupport table entirely -- not marked NO, not marked n/a, absent -- so there is no\nrecorded answer to whether their receivers are typed by any mechanism, while\n244 method rows depend on it. They are now listed 'not assessed' in the ADR\nrather than silently missing. Assessing them is the first step here."  # 893f
    fields: {}  # 893f
- op: discuss  # 8fd0
  at: '2026-08-30T02:31:50Z'  # 8fd0
  by: agent  # 8fd0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8fd0
  clock: 2  # 8fd0
  nonce: 8fd0  # 8fd0
  message: "Measurement 0009 (WI-vapud) re-derived this item's mechanism from the opposite direction and put a volume on it inside the ADR-0049 family. The discriminator is sharper than 'method rows need a typed receiver': a receiver typed from a CONSTRUCTOR or a DECLARED PARAMETER resolves, and a receiver typed from an external function's RETURN VALUE does not resolve at all. Measured, two languages, controlled within the same fixture: go -- 'var r gin.Engine; r.Run()' FIRES, 'r := gin.Default(); r.Run()' emits dst_ref module=None; java -- 'Socket s = new Socket(h,p); s.getInputStream()' FIRES, 'Connection c = DriverManager.getConnection(u); c.prepareStatement(s)' emits dst_ref module=None. Java also types a receiver from a declared parameter (EntityManager em -> em.getReference fires), which is why the java arm looks partly healthy. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ADR-0049 RETAG: all seven go third-party framework launch rows (gin.Engine.Run/RunTLS, fiber.App.Listen/ListenTLS, echo.Echo.Start/StartTLS, grpc.Server.Serve) are INERT in real code, because every one of those frameworks is entered through a constructor call -- gin.Default(), fiber.New(), echo.New(), grpc.NewServer(). Plus java.sql.Connection.prepareStatement/prepareCall. Those nine rows are the concrete near-term payoff of this item, and until it lands they are the rows INV-kanuk should retag LAST. Fixtures: idiom/gofull and idiom/javafull in ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vapud_reach_08292026/."  # 8fd0
- op: discuss  # b317
  at: '2026-08-30T08:49:57Z'  # b317
  by: agent  # b317
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b317
  clock: 3  # b317
  nonce: b317  # b317
  message: "ONE OF THE SEVEN INERT GO FRAMEWORK ROWS IS NOW LIVE, MEASURED, AND IT NEEDED NO SIGNATURE CATALOGUE. This item's discussion records all seven as inert 'because every one of those frameworks is entered through a constructor call -- gin.Default(), fiber.New(), echo.New(), grpc.NewServer()'. That is right about the mechanism and wrong about one of the four names: grpc.NewServer() matches Go's NewXxx convention, which go.py's _type_from_constructor_call already inferred -- it was simply DROPPING THE PACKAGE QUALIFIER, binding a bare 'Server' that resolved to no package. With the qualifier preserved (fixed today under WI-vutav), grpc.NewServer() binds grpc.Server and srv.Serve(lis) classifies.\n\nMEASURED, A/B in one process on jaeger, old branch restored by monkeypatch:\n  net_listen  33 -> 38  (+5), every one google.golang.org/grpc.Server.Serve\n  external module slot  8855 -> 8275  (-580 call edges)\ncaddy in the same run: -148 edges out of the external slot, io tags unchanged.\n\nTHE SPLIT IS EXACTLY THE NAMING CONVENTION, so the residual is precise rather\nthan estimated.  and  are not  -- the heuristic requires\nlen(name) > 3 and an uppercase fourth character -- so gin.Default(), fiber.New()\nand echo.New() are untouched and still inert. SIX of the seven rows still wait on\nthis item, plus java.sql.Connection.prepareStatement/prepareCall. The\nproducing-function -> returned-type table is still the fix for those; it is now\nworth six rows here rather than seven.\n\nWHY THIS DOES NOT SHRINK THE ITEM MUCH. The 820 method-kind rows across nine\nlanguages are unaffected -- a NewXxx heuristic covers Go's naming convention and\nnothing else. It is worth recording only because it moves a NAMED blocker from\n'seven rows, all inert' to 'six', and because the mechanism it fixes (qualifier\ndropped on an inference that otherwise worked) is a different defect from the one\nthis item describes (no inference available at all). Do not let the two merge:\nos.Open still needs this item, because os.Open is not a NewXxx name.\n\nFixtures and the A/B rig: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vutav_reads_08302026/."  # b317
